HST Office: when the system ends the session, store the open documents and reopen them at the next start.

A log out, shut down or restart ended the office in three ways, all losing changes: Windows asked
"Save document?" inside WM_QUERYENDSESSION and ended the process while the question held the log off
(the changes after the last recovery copy were lost, the next start offered the recovery dialog);
Linux SIGTERM (systemd stopping the session; KDE and other desktops) exited at once with the same
result; a GNOME/Cinnamon EndSession stored the documents by the X11 session protocol, which clears
their modified state. All three now run one AutoRecovery job first:
- framework autorecovery.cxx: vnd.sun.star.autorecovery:/doSessionEnd = doSessionRestart without the
  restart: every document is stored to its recovery file, the files are checked, the documents are
  closed keeping their modified state and the session is marked (RecoveryInfo/SessionData). The next
  start reopens them silently, still modified. Without AutoRecovery (--norestore), or if a document
  has no usable recovery file (password, untracked), nothing is closed and the caller keeps its old way.
- vcl win salframe.cxx: WM_QUERYENDSESSION with unsaved documents stores them first (Windows shows
  "There are unsaved documents" as the reason meanwhile), then answers "yes" at once and quits
  (directly: storing disposes the document windows, a shutdown sent through one is dropped). A
  document that could not be stored keeps the save question.
- vcl gtk3 gtkframe.cxx: EndSession/Stop run doSessionEnd first; doSessionSave + doSessionQuietQuit
  only for what it could not take.
- desktop hstsessionend.cxx (new), appinit.cxx (installed when the main loop starts): Linux SIGTERM (not headless or invisible): the signal
  handler only writes to a pipe; a watcher thread posts the session end to the main thread, which
  stores the documents and terminates normally. A document that could not be stored, a veto or no
  end within 20 s end the office at once as before ("Terminated: Signal 15"); repeated SIGTERMs
  (systemd signals every process, oosplash forwards its own) change nothing.
- desktop unx start.c (oosplash): it forwarded SIGTERM and killed soffice.bin after 1 s, cutting the
  store off; it now waits 25 s (soffice.bin ends itself within 20 s, a headless one at once).
License: these upstream files remain under their original license (MPL-2.0); the new files are MPL-2.0.
Test: tests/runtime/check-session-end-linux.py; tests/runtime/check-session-end-gnome.py;
tests/runtime/check-session-end-windows-answer.py; tests/runtime/check-session-end-windows.py.
